That explains it. The admin DN is the demo kirk cert (CN=kirk,OU=client,O=client,L=test, C=de), but nobody mounted kirk.pem into the container. The ls on the host failed because that path only exists inside the container. On the host, the files are in /home/xxujd/elasticsearch_opendistro/.

1. Check the host directory first
bash
ls -l /home/xxujd/elasticsearch_opendistro/

If kirk.pem and kirk-key.pem are there, copy them into the container and skip to step 3. While you're there, look for a docker-compose.yml or other files. The admin password might be sitting in plain text in one of them.

bash
docker cp /home/xxujd/elasticsearch_opendistro/kirk.pem opensearch-node1:/tmp/
docker cp /home/xxujd/elasticsearch_opendistro/kirk-key.pem opensearch-node1:/tmp/
2. If they're not there, extract the demo kirk cert

esnode.pem, root-ca.pem and allow_unsafe_democertificates: true strongly suggest these are the stock demo certs. Confirm that on the host:

bash
openssl x509 -in /home/xxujd/elasticsearch_opendistro/root-ca.pem -noout -subject

If the subject mentions Example Com Inc. Root CA, it's the demo CA. The matching kirk cert and key are embedded in the demo install script that ships in the image. Extract them inside the container:

bash
docker exec -it opensearch-node1 bash
cd /usr/share/opensearch/plugins/opensearch-security/tools

extract() {
  awk -v want="/$1\"" '
    /-----BEGIN/ {buf=""; cap=1}
    cap {l=$0; sub(/^echo "/,"",l); sub(/".*$/,"",l); buf=buf l "\n"}
    /-----END/ {cap=0}
    index($0, want) {printf "%s", buf}
  ' install_demo_configuration.sh > /tmp/$1
}
extract kirk.pem
extract kirk-key.pem

Before using them, verify the subject matches admin_dn, the issuer matches the root CA, and the two hashes are identical (meaning the key matches the cert):

bash
openssl x509 -in /tmp/kirk.pem -noout -subject -issuer
openssl x509 -in /tmp/kirk.pem -noout -modulus | md5sum
openssl rsa  -in /tmp/kirk-key.pem -noout -modulus | md5sum

If the files come out empty, run grep -n kirk install_demo_configuration.sh and copy the blocks by hand.

3. Back up, edit, upload
bash
export JAVA_HOME=/usr/share/opensearch/jdk
cd /usr/share/opensearch/plugins/opensearch-security/tools
CFG=/usr/share/opensearch/config
ADM="-icl -nhnv -cacert $CFG/root-ca.pem -cert /tmp/kirk.pem -key /tmp/kirk-key.pem"

./securityadmin.sh -backup /tmp/secbackup $ADM
./hash.sh -p 'the-real-new-password'
# replace admin's hash in /tmp/secbackup/internal_users.yml
./securityadmin.sh -f /tmp/secbackup/internal_users.yml -t internalusers $ADM

Afterwards, copy the edited file over /home/xxujd/elasticsearch_opendistro/internal_users.yml on the host. That keeps the mounted file in line with what's live, so a future re-init doesn't bring the old (or unknown) password back.

Worth flagging

This is a prod box running the public demo certs, and port 9300 is published on 0.0.0.0. Anyone who can reach that port can do exactly what you're doing now with a publicly available cert, so this is effectively an unauthenticated admin backdoor. At minimum, firewall 9300 (nothing outside the cluster needs it). Replacing the demo PKI is worth putting on the list, along with the 1.2.x upgrade.
